{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790342206,"nonce":"1Je01FRf98lQGzgpR0Xl1fBuamk=","title":"Pool slabs are never returned, and a new slab is written through on refill","message":"pool_alloc.rs refill takes a 64 KB aligned slab from the Rust allocator and threads its free list through every block, touching every page; a slab the sweep finds empty goes on EMPTY_SLABS still dirty and is never freed or advised away.\n\nEvidence (Mac, slab census at exit): retired but resident slabs binary_trees 464 of 591 (29 MB), tokenize 395 (24.7 MB), op_overload 12.4 MB. print(1): 5 to 7 slabs (410 KB zylua, 250 KB zypy). Large blocks go back to libc and do leave the footprint (pipeline peak minus exit 47 MB).\n\nSavings: long-running programs return to live plus budget: binary_trees about 29 MB, tokenize about 25, op_overload about 12 after their peak; 0.25 to 0.4 MB of floor; up to 8 MB for programs touching many size classes.\n\nFix: carve blocks by bump pointer from the unused extent and thread only freed blocks; take slabs from pool-owned 64 KB aligned mmap chunks and madvise (MADV_FREE, MADV_FREE_REUSABLE on macOS) retired slabs beyond the next budget, so a steady churner never pays the fault. Package MEM-gc-heap-return.","files":null}]}